## Why now rather than at close-out

A stale range doesn't error — it returns a **different, plausible-looking section**, and the plan's own
Reading Protocol is what sends agents to those ranges. Three of the misaddressed sections are the ones
about to be read:

- **§2d.5 and §2d.6 are the spec for the commit in flight.** C6c is exactly `fairShareValue` currency
  (§2d.5) + the T1.4 rewrite (§2d.6). Fetching §2d.5 by TOC lands in §2d.4 — which *contains* finding (b),
  about `fairShareValue`, so it reads like the right material while omitting the currency spec and the
  T1.4 instructions entirely. Wrong section, right topic, no error: the hardest kind to notice.
- **§6 is the semantic-pivot greps**, mandatory per CODER-CONVENTIONS §3. Its TOC range returns dev-guide
  prose with no greps in it. A careful coder concludes the plan omitted the grep step and writes a
  gap handoff (wasted cycle); a less careful one skips the grep.
- **§5 is the per-commit dev-guide map.** Its range returns §4's test requirements.

## Ask

Run the prescribed refresh — CONVENTIONS: *"Before handing any plan doc to a coder, run
`bash plans/scripts/toc-refresh.sh <plan-file.md>` … Idempotent."* The C10 fold-ins through `62c37c46`
added content without one. Ideally before the coder next fetches §2d.5/§2d.6/§4/§5/§6.

I did **not** run it myself: the plan is a Type 3 doc and planner-owned, so it goes back to you under the
pre-action gate even though it's a single idempotent script and I can see exactly what it would do.

